{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209976,"nonce":"BVEsOBfMLA98W5Ym9yENvXV6v6I=","title":"**What is wrong.** Storm handling has never been run against a storm. There is no","message":"test, benchmark or manual run that pushes a large Alertmanager batch through\n`POST /api/v1/ingest/alertmanager`. The audit's ingest probing was a 40-request\nburst of small payloads (`review-conformance.md`, ADR 0007 row: \"40-request burst\n→ 40×202, **no 429**\"). Every storm-shaped code path — B17 chunking\n(`internal/ingestion/domain/bounds.go:64`), the shedder\n(`internal/ingestion/api/shed.go`), the storm threshold latch on\n`channels.storm_notice_at` (commit `bf3006b`), the per-thread ordering gate under\nhundreds of queued deliveries — is unexercised at scale.\n\n**Why it matters.** The reason to run oto instead of reading Alertmanager's own\nSlack receiver is that oto is supposed to behave well when 500 alerts fire at\nonce. Nobody knows today whether that batch is accepted, sharded, shed, or\nwhether it opens 500 Slack threads. If it degrades, it degrades on the worst day\nof the customer's quarter, and Alertmanager does not retry a 4xx — the alerts are\nsimply gone (ADR 0007).\n\n**Where.** `internal/ingestion/`, `internal/grouping/service/service.go`\n(`evaluateStorm`), `internal/notification/service/`, `test/load/` (currently a\nlone `doc.go`).\n\n**Done when.** A repeatable load case pushes at least 500 alerts in one batch and\nseveral thousand across a few minutes through a real Postgres, and the following\nare recorded: ingest latency and status codes, number of Slack calls actually\nmade, that exactly one storm notice per channel is emitted, that the ordering gate\ndoes not wedge, and that no delivery is lost or duplicated. Numbers land in the\nrepo so a regression is visible.","files":null}]}